atient is having a manic  episode  of course  none of these were available in  vincent's time  instead  he and other patients were treated with  hydrotherapy  twice a week  in both hot and cold water  the bathtubs  are still in place today  now i take a bath twice a week and stay  in it for two hours  my stomach is infinitely better than a  year ago so as far as i know  i just have to continue  and so vincent settled in and soon  discovered that the serenity promised by  the institution  was often elusive  day and night the air was filled with  shouting and howling as though from a  group of caged animals as he wrote  but he tried to make the best of it  i have a little room with gray green  paper and two water green curtains with  designs of pale roses and thin lines of  blood red  the curtains are actually very pretty  though probably left by some ruined rich  man now deceased  from the same source most likely comes a  very worn armchair  the windows of his room  were barred as in a prison cell but  remarkably vincent only had eyes for the  view beyond  through the iron barred window i see a  square field of wheat in an enclosure  a perspective like a van going  above which i see the sun rise in all  its glory  eventually he began to explore the  asylum itself  including the long corridors of the  monastery and the pretty garden in the  courtyard  with a small fountain in the center  his studies of irises and plants in the  garden  show a notable shift in his palette  the exuberant orange and yellows of the  oral period  have given way to a new emphasis on more  somber colors  vincent was well aware of it  when i dream in my best moments i don't  see striking color effects as much as  halftones  what's interesting  is the painstaking detail with which  vincent painted the irises almost as if  he wants to prove to himself that he is  fully capable of dissecting such a small  subject with such clarity  the frenzy of slashing paint on the  canvas is nowhere in evidence  vincent is working in a more deliberate  fashion  perhaps in an effort to  regain his mental balance  on june 5th  dr perron dean vinson to have  sufficiently recovered to allow him to  roam freely in the immediate vicinity of  the asylum but time and again  vincent was drawn to this hypnotic image  of a field in an enclosure that he had  first seen from his window  he now painted it  at eye level  in the foreground is a wheat field  flattened and laid to waste by a storm  beyond the wall is a gray foliage of  some olive trees  some cottages and the hills  and on top of the canvas a large white  and gray cloud drowning in the azure sky  with the onset of summer  and the scintillating heat rising from  the earth vincent's view of nature began  to change  under his hands the orchards and  cypresses were gradually transformed as  if captured by a nervous intensity  it seems as if all of creation is caught  up in a powerful storm the same storm  that some believe was raging in vinson's  mind  the skies the fields the torch-like  cypresses all seem possessed by this  strange cosmic energy  the earth is in turmoil  nature  in flames  but when we look deeper  it seems that this distortion is  deliberate for the composition itself  still unfolds along classical and  symmetrical lines  [Music]  the violent colors too seem calculated  to balance each other out  the gold of ripe wheat versus the cold  blue of the sky  the shimmering green of cypresses versus  the neutral gray of the olive trees  so he made a few paintings in the spring  summer early summer and he  had an attack  and then he started again painting in  september for him olive grove was a very  important symbol as well to stay in the  south of france and cyprus's olive  groves  all peel the  and so you find a lot of these typical  uh southern french symbols in a way for  him they were symbols in his paintings  that's why the suggestion that these  tortured forms were a direct expression  of vincent's tormented mind  is not plausible  [Music]  instead when vincent looked at these  landscapes he truly experienced them as  nature in motion  and that's the way he painted it  in this reaper  a vague figure fighting like a devil in  the midst of the heat to get to the end  of his task  i see the image of death  in the sense that humanity  might be the wheat he's reaping  it is during this time that vincent  painted the masterpiece from his sorremi  period  which today is the pride of the museum  of modern art in new york  the starry night  as we saw previously vincent was deeply  interested in the subtle tints of the  night and he tried to capture them with  any color other than black  and now from his window at the asylum he  could see a night in all of its spectral  splendor  without any gas light seeping into his  view  i don't think we'll ever be able to see  what vincent saw  since the invention of electric light  our towns and cities have forever washed  away the purity of night as the 19th  century saw it  back then  on a clear night you could see the milky  way in all of its splendor arcing across  the sky on a bed of crystal clear stars  that's what vinson saw and that's what  he painted in this gorgeous painting  the awesome majesty  of a summer night in provence  once again he used a very traditional  composition  a freeze-like horizontal view of the  village with the alpil mountains beyond  anchored on the left by towering  cypresses  and on the right  by the crescent moon  [Music]  this morning at some time before sunrise  i saw the country from my window with  nothing but the morning star  [Music]  there are a few strange notes in this  painting  for example the church with its  exaggerated tall spire  there is no such church in saarimi and  in fact such spires are a typical motif  of the north so what vincent did is  insert a memory from his time in nunen  which shows once again how much his  years in holland were on his mind as he  spent time in the south  it 